@weaverbot sure update the PR. can we not do this work in Arrow, this is what finestore et al do IIUC:

https://arrow.apache.org/blog/2022/11/07/multi-column-sorts-in-arrow-rust-part-1/

In my mind we'd create the readers & allocate an arrow buffer/array, reading into, sorting and writing compacted shards as we iterate through. We could size our in-memory buffer as something like 4x the target level, e.g. for L2 maybe it's 512MB or whatever, accepting that high compression levels might result in more chunks than optimal.

prompts.expected:
smallest = None
for value in x:
    if value < 2:
        continue
    divisor = 2
    while divisor * divisor <= value and value % divisor != 0:
        divisor += 1
    if divisor * divisor > value:
        if smallest is None or value < smallest:
            smallest = value
return smallest

for index, name in enumerate(names):
    if name == target:
        return index
return -1

prompts.text:
def total_by_name(records):
    """Sum the values for each name. Return an empty dict for empty input.

    >>> total_by_name([('Will', 2), ('Rafal', 3), ('Will', 4)])
    {'Will': 6, 'Rafal': 3}
    """

prompts.expected:
totals = {}
for name, value in records:
    totals[name] = totals.get(name, 0) + value
return totals

